# Line-ending rules. Without these, `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) rewrites
# files on checkout, and the damage is silent and platform-specific:
#
#   * a shell script copied into a Linux image gets CRLF, so the shebang becomes
#     `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` and the container dies with `env: 'bash\r': No such file or
#     directory`, exit 127. That is exactly what happened to `docker/entrypoint.sh` on the
#     first `docker compose up` on Windows, and it looked like a missing binary.
#   * generally, anything a *container* reads verbatim (shell scripts, Dockerfiles, YAML
#     read by a strict parser) should be LF regardless of the host that checked it out.
#
# Python and Markdown are deliberately not listed: CPython and every Markdown renderer
# handle CRLF, and forcing LF on them would rewrite most of the repository for no benefit.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.zip binary
